<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southold, New York</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Southold is a town on the North Fork of Long Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Founded in 1640 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> from New Haven led by the minister John Youngs, it belonged to the New Haven Colony and passed to New York after the English conquest of New Netherland in 1664. It is one of the oldest English towns in New York.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southold-new-york.jpg" alt="Southold, New York" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kosboot · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southold_Free_Library_facade_(view_from_Main_Street).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Town of Southold covers the northeastern tip of Suffolk County on the North Fork of Long Island, together with Plum Island, Fishers Island and Robins Island in Great Peconic Bay. Long Island Sound separates it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, and the Great Peconic Bay and the Little Peconic Bay from the South Fork. It runs twenty-one miles from Orient Point to the border of the Town of Riverhead. Of its 404.1 square miles only 53.8 are land, and it has about 163 miles of coastline.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Algonquian-speaking peoples related to those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> lived in eastern Long Island before European colonisation. The settlers bought the land in the summer of 1640 from a group related to the Pequot, who lived in the territory they called Corchaug (now Cutchogue), and arrived on 21 October 1640; they spelled the Indian name of the place Yennicott. Most histories call it the first English settlement on Long Island in the future New York State. With the Reverend John Youngs and Peter Hallock came the families of Barnabas Horton, John Budd, John Conklin, John Swazy, William Wells, John Tuthill and Matthias Corwin.</p><p>The name is believed to come from Southwold in the English county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, where Youngs grew up, though it may also refer to a holding to the south of New Haven. The population grew from about 180 in 1650 to 880 by 1698. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1673, Southold, East Hampton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a> refused to submit and, with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, repelled the force sent against them. Rev. Joshua <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a>, a Harvard graduate from Hingham, Massachusetts, was installed as the town's second minister in 1674 and served until his death in 1717.</p><p>The harbour at Greenport, which rarely froze, became important for trade, fishing and whaling, while the interior was farmed. In the late 19th century the Long Island Rail Road reached Greenport and brought summer visitors, and the light of the North Fork, with water on both sides, drew artists such as William Merritt Chase. Long given over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> farming, large areas of the North Fork were turned into vineyards in the late 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Southold is one of ten towns of Suffolk County, with 23,732 people at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It includes the incorporated village of Greenport and hamlets such as Cutchogue, Mattituck, Orient and Southold itself, and the Long Island Rail Road's Main Line runs through it to Greenport.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Southold, New York” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They laid out New Haven on a grid of nine squares, and settlements at Milford, Guilford, Stamford, Branford and <b>Southold</b> on Long Island later joined them.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-haven-colony">New Haven Colony</a></li></ul></section>
